 ## Overview
-Frequency Domain Directional Shading is a local illumination technique for volume rendering that computes diffuse shading entirely in the frequency domain, avoiding spatial domain differentiation [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of applying directional lighting cues to volume data without the computational cost or nonlinearity issues of spatial domain methods. Unlike [Simplified Phong Lighting Model](885-simplified-phong-lighting-model.md) and [Lambert Shading with Shadow Rays](1133-lambert-shading-with-shadow-rays.md), which operate in the spatial domain or require shadow rays, this method leverages the property that spatial differentiation corresponds to multiplication by a linear ramp in the frequency domain [S2, unofficial]. It differs from [Gradient-based Shading with Phase Functions](1020-gradient-based-shading-with-phase-functions.md) by using a hemispherical light source model to maintain linearity, enabling efficient frequency domain computation [S1, S2, unofficial].
